00:15:09.310 --> 00:15:12.220
This is a very short answer.

00:15:12.220 --> 00:15:16.210
They use the machine learning
model called old encoder

00:15:16.210 --> 00:15:18.640
to detect abnormal data.

00:15:18.640 --> 00:15:22.840
This is a very simplified
diagram of the old encoder.

00:15:22.840 --> 00:15:28.190
But actually, the mechanism
of old encoder is very simple.

00:15:28.190 --> 00:15:31.480
It is a machine learning model
to regenerate the same data

00:15:31.480 --> 00:15:34.370
as input on the outside.

00:15:34.370 --> 00:15:36.190
So we try and do some
action on a model

00:15:36.190 --> 00:15:40.780
to minimize that delta
between input and output.

00:15:40.780 --> 00:15:43.780
And the question
is, how and why we

00:15:43.780 --> 00:15:48.210
can use this machine learning
model to detect abnormal data?

00:15:48.210 --> 00:15:52.660
To explain that, I prepared
a simple, tiny toy model

00:15:52.660 --> 00:15:55.300
using the [INAUDIBLE] data set.

00:15:55.300 --> 00:15:58.495
[INAUDIBLE] data set is,
as you know, the collection

00:15:58.495 --> 00:16:01.000
of handwritten digit images.

00:16:01.000 --> 00:16:02.920
We barely know if
we use this data set

00:16:02.920 --> 00:16:06.050
to explain some basic
mechanism of machine learning.

00:16:06.050 --> 00:16:09.740
So this is a kind of
"hello world" example.

00:16:09.740 --> 00:16:12.130
But anyway, I will
show you some code.

00:16:24.770 --> 00:16:27.060
OK, can you turn to
the demonstration?

00:16:27.060 --> 00:16:28.140
OK.

00:16:28.140 --> 00:16:29.790
This is a collab notebook.

00:16:29.790 --> 00:16:33.510
I implemented a tiny toy
model on this notebook,

00:16:33.510 --> 00:16:37.000
so let me go through
this notebook.

00:16:37.000 --> 00:16:41.340
First, I import
some module, and I

00:16:41.340 --> 00:16:44.160
download the
[INAUDIBLE] data set,

00:16:44.160 --> 00:16:47.970
and I define the machine
learning model, old encoder.

00:16:47.970 --> 00:16:49.880
I don't explain
details of the code,

00:16:49.880 --> 00:16:52.050
but if you know
about [INAUDIBLE],,

00:16:52.050 --> 00:16:54.430
you can understand
what's happening.

00:16:54.430 --> 00:16:58.860
I define the very simple neural
network model with six layers,

00:16:58.860 --> 00:17:02.190
and I will train this
machine learning model

00:17:02.190 --> 00:17:05.970
to minimize the delta
between input and output

00:17:05.970 --> 00:17:13.079
by using the MSE, mean squares
error, as a loss function.

00:17:13.079 --> 00:17:18.240
And I will train this machine
learning model, initially,

00:17:18.240 --> 00:17:21.690
with just a little data, only
for a single [? epoch. ?]

00:17:21.690 --> 00:17:23.609
And this season is out.

00:17:23.609 --> 00:17:27.630
The top side is the input
data, and the bottom side

00:17:27.630 --> 00:17:29.460
is the output data.

00:17:29.460 --> 00:17:31.500
You can see that
this model is now

00:17:31.500 --> 00:17:35.160
trying very hard
like a small child

00:17:35.160 --> 00:17:38.880
to re-generate the same
data, but it's not perfect.

00:17:38.880 --> 00:17:42.450
So I will keep training.

00:17:42.450 --> 00:17:45.600
I trained this model
with some additional data

00:17:45.600 --> 00:17:48.790
for more [? epochs. ?]
And you will

00:17:48.790 --> 00:17:54.920
see the result. Like this one.

00:17:54.920 --> 00:17:56.900
OK, it's far better now.

00:17:56.900 --> 00:17:59.240
And now, I will use
this trend model

00:17:59.240 --> 00:18:02.190
to detect some abnormal data.

00:18:08.050 --> 00:18:13.020
OK, I draw some number
on this white canvas.

00:18:13.020 --> 00:18:19.820
And first, I try some simple
one, just number zero.

00:18:19.820 --> 00:18:22.730
And you see the
result. The output

00:18:22.730 --> 00:18:25.830
is very similar to the input.

00:18:25.830 --> 00:18:28.100
And now, I try
something different.

00:18:31.010 --> 00:18:36.170
I draw, not a
number, but a letter,

00:18:36.170 --> 00:18:41.485
B. Can you guess
what would happen?

00:18:41.485 --> 00:18:42.610
AUDIENCE: They [INAUDIBLE].

00:18:42.610 --> 00:18:47.710
ETSUJI NAKAI: Yes the output
is different from letter B.

00:18:47.710 --> 00:18:51.730
It looks like number
eight or number three.

00:18:51.730 --> 00:18:54.850
Yeah, this is because I trained
this machine learning model

00:18:54.850 --> 00:18:58.060
with only numbers,
so it always tried

00:18:58.060 --> 00:19:01.730
to re-generate some
data similar to numbers.

00:19:01.730 --> 00:19:04.330
So in this case, the
output is totally

00:19:04.330 --> 00:19:06.350
different from the input.

00:19:06.350 --> 00:19:09.320
So by comparing the
input and the output,

00:19:09.320 --> 00:19:12.700
you can say that the
delta is very large.

00:19:12.700 --> 00:19:16.100
This input is different
from training data.

00:19:16.100 --> 00:19:19.040
This is something
different from the numbers.

00:19:19.040 --> 00:19:21.070
This is a basic mechanism.

00:19:21.070 --> 00:19:25.310
Can you go back to
the presentation?

00:19:25.310 --> 00:19:27.710
OK.

00:19:27.710 --> 00:19:30.680
So once you train this
machine learning model

00:19:30.680 --> 00:19:33.740
with no more data, you can use
this machine learning model

00:19:33.740 --> 00:19:39.130
to detect abnormal data by
comparing the input and output.

00:19:39.130 --> 00:19:40.640
Very good.

00:19:40.640 --> 00:19:46.190
However, now you are faced with
technical challenge number one,

00:19:46.190 --> 00:19:48.560
no labeled data.

00:19:48.560 --> 00:19:52.400
As I told you, you need to train
this machine learning model

00:19:52.400 --> 00:19:55.100
with only normal data.

00:19:55.100 --> 00:19:58.790
To do that, you have to
split up your whole data set

00:19:58.790 --> 00:20:03.110
into two parts, normal
part and abnormal part.

00:20:03.110 --> 00:20:05.430
How can we do that?

00:20:05.430 --> 00:20:08.610
We need a machine
learning model for that.

00:20:08.610 --> 00:20:11.450
So this is a chicken
and egg problem.

00:20:11.450 --> 00:20:15.740
I think this is a very common
problem in the gaming industry.

00:20:15.740 --> 00:20:19.280
Generally, they
have a lot of data,

00:20:19.280 --> 00:20:22.460
but they are not well organized.

00:20:22.460 --> 00:20:26.420
They need some tricks
to organize those data

00:20:26.420 --> 00:20:29.690
so that they can use that
data for machine learning.

00:20:29.690 --> 00:20:31.790
However, sometimes
you need machine

00:20:31.790 --> 00:20:34.770
learning itself to do that.

00:20:34.770 --> 00:20:39.890
So we had several discussions of
how we can solve this problem.

00:20:39.890 --> 00:20:45.110
And our solution is
surprisingly simple.

00:20:45.110 --> 00:20:49.910
We used the whole data,
including abnormal ones,

00:20:49.910 --> 00:20:52.820
to train this machine
learning model.

00:20:52.820 --> 00:20:57.470
And naturally, we have several
assumptions behind that.

00:20:57.470 --> 00:21:01.130
One of the biggest
assumption is that the amount

00:21:01.130 --> 00:21:04.550
of abnormal data
is very, very small

00:21:04.550 --> 00:21:06.780
compared to the normal data.

00:21:06.780 --> 00:21:09.380
So the inference
of abnormal data

00:21:09.380 --> 00:21:14.180
would be simply overwritten by
the huge amount of normal data.

00:21:14.180 --> 00:21:16.400
Of course, this is
just an assumption.

00:21:16.400 --> 00:21:18.580
We have to verify that.

00:21:18.580 --> 00:21:22.160
We tried several different
machine learning models

00:21:22.160 --> 00:21:24.110
to check our assumption.

00:21:24.110 --> 00:21:27.560
And finally, it actually worked.

00:21:27.560 --> 00:21:29.690
Sounds good?

00:21:29.690 --> 00:21:35.270
I guess some of you are now
thinking, this is too simple.

00:21:35.270 --> 00:21:39.780
This is different from
what I expected to hear.

00:21:39.780 --> 00:21:40.280
Yeah.

00:21:40.280 --> 00:21:46.970
However, in reality,
the most important part

00:21:46.970 --> 00:21:50.090
of applying machine
learning to your business

00:21:50.090 --> 00:21:55.070
is to choose the best and
simplest machine learning model

00:21:55.070 --> 00:21:57.410
to solve your problem.

00:21:57.410 --> 00:21:59.720
It is a very tricky part.

00:21:59.720 --> 00:22:02.690
To do that, you
have to understand

00:22:02.690 --> 00:22:07.100
variety of machine learning
models, pros and cons of them.

00:22:07.100 --> 00:22:10.520
And you need to choose
the best and simplest one.

00:22:10.520 --> 00:22:12.550
It's very hard.

00:22:12.550 --> 00:22:15.490
And that's why I'm here.

00:22:15.490 --> 00:22:17.230
I can help you for that.

00:22:17.230 --> 00:22:20.770
I actually have
Netmarble for that.

00:22:20.770 --> 00:22:26.080
Anyway, before going to explain
another technical challenge,

00:22:26.080 --> 00:22:29.710
I want to explain the production
use case of this machine

00:22:29.710 --> 00:22:31.070
learning model.

00:22:31.070 --> 00:22:35.410
Now, they re-train this machine
learning model every day

00:22:35.410 --> 00:22:38.950
using that fresh data
in past two days.

00:22:38.950 --> 00:22:41.200
And after re-training the
machine learning model,

00:22:41.200 --> 00:22:45.070
they deploy the trained model
to the production system.

00:22:45.070 --> 00:22:47.680
And then, they used
the similar time gaming

00:22:47.680 --> 00:22:51.220
[? log ?] data to
detect abnormal users.

00:22:51.220 --> 00:22:55.330
And then, they create a
list of suspicious users

00:22:55.330 --> 00:22:58.490
and send the list to
the operational team.

00:22:58.490 --> 00:23:00.670
And finally, the
operational team

00:23:00.670 --> 00:23:03.700
does manual further
investigation

00:23:03.700 --> 00:23:08.230
to have complete evidence
that some users did actually

00:23:08.230 --> 00:23:09.550
something that wrong.

00:23:09.550 --> 00:23:12.490
And then, they decide
the appropriate action

00:23:12.490 --> 00:23:15.220
against those bad users.

00:23:15.220 --> 00:23:19.030
And again, you may
have some questions.

00:23:19.030 --> 00:23:22.150
First question is, why
we have to re-train

00:23:22.150 --> 00:23:24.070
the model every day.

00:23:24.070 --> 00:23:28.010
Because the data
freshness matters.

00:23:28.010 --> 00:23:33.240
In the world of online
gaming, especially in MMORPG,

00:23:33.240 --> 00:23:38.210
the user behavior always
changes due to some reasons.

00:23:38.210 --> 00:23:41.100
Sometimes they have
some, in gaming events,

00:23:41.100 --> 00:23:44.880
the latest new game features
and characters every day.

00:23:44.880 --> 00:23:51.750
And even without those
elements, maturity of users

00:23:51.750 --> 00:23:55.680
naturally influence the
behavior of those users.

00:23:55.680 --> 00:23:59.690
So it's a kind of natural
evolution in the virtual gaming

00:23:59.690 --> 00:24:00.770
world.

00:24:00.770 --> 00:24:03.740
So to keep up with these
changing realities,

00:24:03.740 --> 00:24:08.450
we have to re-train the model
every day with fresh data.

00:24:08.450 --> 00:24:14.470
And the second question is,
why manual investigation?

00:24:14.470 --> 00:24:18.100
Isn't it machine learning
to automate everything?

00:24:18.100 --> 00:24:23.750
Isn't it a good idea to
automatically log bad users?

00:24:23.750 --> 00:24:29.430
Unfortunately, it's not a
good idea for several reasons.

00:24:29.430 --> 00:24:34.540
One of those reasons is
to avoid false positives.

00:24:34.540 --> 00:24:39.990
It's really bad if we block
some normal users mistakenly.

00:24:39.990 --> 00:24:42.360
It's the last thing
we want to do.

00:24:42.360 --> 00:24:45.150
And the second
reason is to decide

00:24:45.150 --> 00:24:49.000
the appropriate actions
against bad users,

00:24:49.000 --> 00:24:51.690
it's sometimes very tricky.

00:24:51.690 --> 00:24:56.280
Suppose that we found several
users who did something wrong,

00:24:56.280 --> 00:24:58.560
just one time from curiosity.

00:24:58.560 --> 00:25:01.800
And what if they're
a very loyal user?

00:25:01.800 --> 00:25:04.200
They spent a lot of
time for that game.

00:25:04.200 --> 00:25:06.630
They spent a lot of money.

00:25:06.630 --> 00:25:10.090
In that case, simply
blocking those users

00:25:10.090 --> 00:25:13.780
is not a right
business decision.

00:25:13.780 --> 00:25:17.010
So they have to
understand the real nature

00:25:17.010 --> 00:25:18.990
of those bad behaviors.

00:25:18.990 --> 00:25:22.500
They have to understand the
real nature of those users

00:25:22.500 --> 00:25:26.190
to decide the appropriate
action against them.

00:25:26.190 --> 00:25:29.790
That's why they need a
manual investigation.

00:25:29.790 --> 00:25:34.350
And the last reason is
somewhat interesting.

00:25:34.350 --> 00:25:36.690
By doing manual
investigation, they

00:25:36.690 --> 00:25:42.450
can understand some new
patterns of abnormal behaviors

00:25:42.450 --> 00:25:46.590
and apply that knowledge to the
existing learning based model

00:25:46.590 --> 00:25:48.540
to improve them.

00:25:48.540 --> 00:25:51.660
You may have thought that once
we created a machine learning

00:25:51.660 --> 00:25:57.090
model, we can throw away all the
existing old rule based model.

00:25:57.090 --> 00:25:59.040
It is not the case.

00:25:59.040 --> 00:26:01.690
In some cases, rule
based model works better

00:26:01.690 --> 00:26:03.270
than machine learning.

00:26:03.270 --> 00:26:04.770
It is a reality.

00:26:04.770 --> 00:26:08.160
So combining machine learning
and a rule based model

00:26:08.160 --> 00:26:11.220
is the ideal solution.

00:26:11.220 --> 00:26:13.620
And in this
particular case, they

00:26:13.620 --> 00:26:16.770
can get new insights
about the new patterns

00:26:16.770 --> 00:26:19.830
of abnormal behaviors
because machine learning

00:26:19.830 --> 00:26:23.850
is very good at finding
new patterns automatically.

00:26:23.850 --> 00:26:26.040
And then, they can
use that new finding

00:26:26.040 --> 00:26:29.010
to improve the existing
rule based system.

00:26:29.010 --> 00:26:32.840
So this is a very
good virtual cycle.

00:26:32.840 --> 00:26:33.890
OK.

00:26:33.890 --> 00:26:39.650
Now, we are faced with the
critical challenge number two.

00:26:39.650 --> 00:26:42.440
It is model explainability.

00:26:42.440 --> 00:26:44.190
What does it mean?

00:26:44.190 --> 00:26:46.050
Because the
operational team needs

00:26:46.050 --> 00:26:51.190
to do some manual investigation,
they need some hint.

00:26:51.190 --> 00:26:52.710
Why did this machine
learning model

00:26:52.710 --> 00:26:56.790
detect these users as abnormal?

00:26:56.790 --> 00:27:01.040
Without any reason, they can't
do the manual investigation

00:27:01.040 --> 00:27:02.220
effectively.

00:27:02.220 --> 00:27:05.050
They need some hint from
the machine learning model.

00:27:05.050 --> 00:27:06.930
This is a problem
that we generally

00:27:06.930 --> 00:27:09.990
call model explainability.

00:27:09.990 --> 00:27:15.030
And to solve this problem,
the AI team in Netmarble

00:27:15.030 --> 00:27:18.510
applied the feature engineering.

00:27:18.510 --> 00:27:21.930
It means instead of
using the original log

00:27:21.930 --> 00:27:24.510
data to train the
machine learning model,

00:27:24.510 --> 00:27:28.080
they combined those
log data, log features,

00:27:28.080 --> 00:27:30.630
to create some new features.

00:27:30.630 --> 00:27:34.260
Those are interpretable
and explainable.

00:27:34.260 --> 00:27:37.050
And then, they used
those engineering

00:27:37.050 --> 00:27:40.360
features to train the
machine learning model.

00:27:40.360 --> 00:27:45.150
And after the training, they can
compare the delta between input

00:27:45.150 --> 00:27:48.630
and output for each feature.

00:27:48.630 --> 00:27:52.410
If some specific feature
has a large delta,

00:27:52.410 --> 00:27:56.430
it is exactly the reason why
this machine learning model

00:27:56.430 --> 00:27:59.920
detected this user as abnormal.

00:27:59.920 --> 00:28:03.060
And this feature
is interpretable.

00:28:03.060 --> 00:28:05.610
Operational team can
use this information

00:28:05.610 --> 00:28:10.300
to do further manual
investigation effectively.

00:28:10.300 --> 00:28:14.880
And this is the overview of
the feature engineering the AI

00:28:14.880 --> 00:28:17.310
team in Netmarble applied.

00:28:17.310 --> 00:28:21.060
The original data is
naturally a time freeze data

00:28:21.060 --> 00:28:23.370
because this is gaming log data.

00:28:23.370 --> 00:28:26.340
And they aggregated
this time freeze data

00:28:26.340 --> 00:28:31.260
with 60 minutes time window
to create new interpretable

00:28:31.260 --> 00:28:32.730
features.

00:28:32.730 --> 00:28:36.990
And you can see some
functions or formulas

00:28:36.990 --> 00:28:42.130
they used to aggregate, to
create interpretable features.

00:28:42.130 --> 00:28:46.960
Unfortunately, details of
these features or formulas

00:28:46.960 --> 00:28:49.020
are confidential.

00:28:49.020 --> 00:28:52.650
These are very, very important
intellectual property

00:28:52.650 --> 00:28:53.520
of Netmarble.

00:28:53.520 --> 00:28:56.410
They cannot disclose everything.

00:28:56.410 --> 00:28:59.220
But if you have some
knowledge in data science,

00:28:59.220 --> 00:29:02.550
or at least statistics,
you can guess

00:29:02.550 --> 00:29:08.110
what they are doing to create
some interpretable features.

00:29:08.110 --> 00:29:10.580
OK.

00:29:10.580 --> 00:29:13.280
And this is the
last slide for me.

00:29:13.280 --> 00:29:18.140
Instead of using the standard
normal, old encoder model,

00:29:18.140 --> 00:29:23.000
they decided to use stacked
denoising autoencoder

00:29:23.000 --> 00:29:25.790
to detect abnormal users.

00:29:25.790 --> 00:29:27.650
I don't have much
time to explain

00:29:27.650 --> 00:29:33.080
what is denoising autoencoder,
but I will give some keywords.

00:29:33.080 --> 00:29:38.960
The most important keyword
is spill over effect.

00:29:38.960 --> 00:29:42.050
By using the
denoising feature, you

00:29:42.050 --> 00:29:47.810
can avoid the spill over
effect of standard old encoder.

00:29:47.810 --> 00:29:50.750
And by avoiding the
spill over effect,

00:29:50.750 --> 00:29:56.390
you can improve the
explainability of the model.

00:29:56.390 --> 00:29:58.370
If you are really
interested in that,

00:29:58.370 --> 00:30:02.030
you can search some
academic papers

00:30:02.030 --> 00:30:04.940
with the key word,
denoising autoencoder,

00:30:04.940 --> 00:30:08.210
and the spill over effect.

00:30:08.210 --> 00:30:12.800
Basically, by avoiding
a spill over effect,

00:30:12.800 --> 00:30:16.800
you can specifically
identify the feature,

00:30:16.800 --> 00:30:25.480
which affects the delta
between input and output.

00:30:25.480 --> 00:30:29.660
Please search some academic
papers for details.

00:30:29.660 --> 00:30:30.170
OK.

00:30:30.170 --> 00:30:32.720
Anyway, now Jennifer
from Netmarble

00:30:32.720 --> 00:30:36.290
will talk about some technical
details of their machine

00:30:36.290 --> 00:30:40.580
learning pipeline, and how
we have collaborated together

00:30:40.580 --> 00:30:43.880
to achieve the actual
business result.

00:30:43.880 --> 00:30:45.770
Please welcome
Jennifer to the stage.

00:30:45.770 --> 00:30:49.249
[APPLAUSE]

00:30:54.220 --> 00:30:56.100
JENNIFER OH: Thank you
for having me, Etsuji.

00:30:56.100 --> 00:30:57.070
Hello, everyone.

00:30:57.070 --> 00:30:58.800
My name is Jennifer Oh.

00:30:58.800 --> 00:31:00.720
I am an ML engineer
in Netmarble,

00:31:00.720 --> 00:31:05.110
and I was also a key member of
[INAUDIBLE] detection project.

00:31:05.110 --> 00:31:07.560
I'm here to share two
kinds of challenges we had

00:31:07.560 --> 00:31:08.610
and how we solved them.

00:31:11.220 --> 00:31:14.010
Before we start, there's
a little dinosaur

00:31:14.010 --> 00:31:16.900
on the right top of the screen.

00:31:16.900 --> 00:31:21.470
You might want to check how the
emotion changes by each slide.

00:31:21.470 --> 00:31:24.930
Anyways, so the first
challenge was this.

00:31:24.930 --> 00:31:28.200
We have so many game titles.

00:31:28.200 --> 00:31:31.650
And these game titles have
unique user behaviors.

00:31:31.650 --> 00:31:33.120
As you can see on
the screen, there

00:31:33.120 --> 00:31:39.300
are some MMORPG games, adventure
RPG games, and also others.

00:31:39.300 --> 00:31:43.060
And these games have a
very different data schema

00:31:43.060 --> 00:31:46.560
in their distribution, since
all the games were made

00:31:46.560 --> 00:31:50.090
by our subsidiary companies.

00:31:50.090 --> 00:31:55.510
So therefore, we could not train
all the data into one model.

00:31:55.510 --> 00:32:00.290
That means we needed at least
one model product per game.

00:32:00.290 --> 00:32:03.430
That's a huge amount
of model products.

00:32:03.430 --> 00:32:07.480
So we thought that we needed
a standardized platform that

00:32:07.480 --> 00:32:10.810
could handle multiple
model products.

00:32:10.810 --> 00:32:16.300
So the solution for
this was simply GCP.

00:32:16.300 --> 00:32:21.760
Using GCP, we could
standardize this whole system.

00:32:21.760 --> 00:32:23.995
Let's check out them in detail.

00:32:26.600 --> 00:32:30.950
So using GCP, we did not
have to manage the machines

00:32:30.950 --> 00:32:33.700
and services since
they are all managed.

00:32:33.700 --> 00:32:37.820
And we did not have to make
new API functions since most

00:32:37.820 --> 00:32:39.710
of them are already made.

00:32:39.710 --> 00:32:43.550
So even if we had
new game titles,

00:32:43.550 --> 00:32:47.660
which require new functions,
we did not have to make them.

00:32:47.660 --> 00:32:52.250
Also, it was easy
to change the system

00:32:52.250 --> 00:32:55.850
scale, which is very important.

00:32:55.850 --> 00:32:59.180
Whether you want to use
distributed environment or not,

00:32:59.180 --> 00:33:03.510
or no matter how large
scale you want to use,

00:33:03.510 --> 00:33:05.360
we did not have
to change anything

00:33:05.360 --> 00:33:11.360
except a line in your config
file or the CLI command.

00:33:11.360 --> 00:33:14.060
Let's say suddenly,
a game's DAU has

00:33:14.060 --> 00:33:19.080
doubled in a day, which
might happen in a few games.

00:33:19.080 --> 00:33:22.170
So the data size
would be doubled too.

00:33:22.170 --> 00:33:25.980
In that case, normally we
have to add some new machines,

00:33:25.980 --> 00:33:28.800
do some setups, and test.

00:33:28.800 --> 00:33:31.830
But in our case, we
don't have any worries.

00:33:31.830 --> 00:33:34.530
Like, what's the
big deal about it?

00:33:34.530 --> 00:33:37.350
All we have to do
was to just change

00:33:37.350 --> 00:33:41.990
the number of nodes
from 10 to 20.

00:33:41.990 --> 00:33:46.460
So with these reasons, we
were able to standardize

00:33:46.460 --> 00:33:47.390
this whole system.

00:33:51.820 --> 00:33:56.010
So let's check out our
system architecture.

00:33:56.010 --> 00:33:59.450
As you can see on the
screen, all the game data

00:33:59.450 --> 00:34:01.740
was saved on BigQuery.

00:34:01.740 --> 00:34:05.430
So we use Dataflow to
extract data from BigQuery

00:34:05.430 --> 00:34:07.800
and do pre-processing.

00:34:07.800 --> 00:34:12.060
And all the pre-processed data
would be saved on [? GCS. ?]

00:34:12.060 --> 00:34:18.690
Using the CSV files, we run
a training job on ML engine.

00:34:18.690 --> 00:34:22.350
When the training job is done,
the exporting model will be

00:34:22.350 --> 00:34:27.750
saved on [? GCS. ?] So using
the files saved on [? GCS, ?] we

00:34:27.750 --> 00:34:31.320
can register the model on
an engine so that we can use

00:34:31.320 --> 00:34:33.523
as prediction.

00:34:33.523 --> 00:34:35.190
Let's move on to the
production process.

00:34:38.659 --> 00:34:41.570
So starting from the
preprocessed files,

00:34:41.570 --> 00:34:43.830
which is same as
the training part,

00:34:43.830 --> 00:34:46.770
we run a prediction
job on ML engine.

00:34:46.770 --> 00:34:49.330
And when the
prediction job is done,

00:34:49.330 --> 00:34:53.469
the predicted results would
be saved on [? GCS. ?]

00:34:53.469 --> 00:34:57.070
And we can just upload
the CSV files to BigQuery,

00:34:57.070 --> 00:35:00.310
and then send the predicted
results to our customers, which

00:35:00.310 --> 00:35:03.520
are the operational team.

00:35:03.520 --> 00:35:08.650
So all this process is
also scheduled by Jenkins.

00:35:08.650 --> 00:35:14.270
The processes are run every
day, or maybe even every hour.

00:35:14.270 --> 00:35:16.810
So guesstimate how
many people we need

00:35:16.810 --> 00:35:19.810
to manage this whole system.

00:35:19.810 --> 00:35:23.760
Just one or maybe even less.

00:35:23.760 --> 00:35:26.570
So if we want to apply
an anomaly detection

00:35:26.570 --> 00:35:30.980
to a new game, all we
have to do is to do data

00:35:30.980 --> 00:35:36.020
EDA and some feature selection,
since all the game data are all

00:35:36.020 --> 00:35:37.800
different.

00:35:37.800 --> 00:35:42.950
And we might do some preparation
for the data pre-processing,

00:35:42.950 --> 00:35:45.470
and change the model
parameters, and it's

00:35:45.470 --> 00:35:49.000
ready for the production.

00:35:49.000 --> 00:35:51.060
So that's it for
the first challenge

00:35:51.060 --> 00:35:54.100
we had, and let's move
on to the next challenge.

00:35:56.880 --> 00:36:00.140
So this is our next
challenge we had.

00:36:00.140 --> 00:36:03.900
So we wanted good
model performance.

00:36:03.900 --> 00:36:08.360
So in order to do that, we had
to read more papers and blogs,

00:36:08.360 --> 00:36:12.390
and run some tests
with multiple models.

00:36:12.390 --> 00:36:15.200
However, we were
running out of time,

00:36:15.200 --> 00:36:19.650
and we did not have
enough human resources.

00:36:19.650 --> 00:36:23.050
So we chose to
collaborate with Google.

00:36:23.050 --> 00:36:25.050
Let's check them in detail.

00:36:27.870 --> 00:36:29.910
So the first thing
we did was having

00:36:29.910 --> 00:36:33.050
workshop with Etsuji Sensei.

00:36:33.050 --> 00:36:37.310
So we explained our
situation and our problem,

00:36:37.310 --> 00:36:40.940
and he shared his
thoughts and ideas.

00:36:40.940 --> 00:36:43.340
So we did architecture
brainstorming together

00:36:43.340 --> 00:36:46.670
and model algorithm
brainstorming together.

00:36:46.670 --> 00:36:50.510
And after a few days, he
gave us a fantastic idea

00:36:50.510 --> 00:36:54.110
about unwanted detection, which
our current system is mostly

00:36:54.110 --> 00:36:55.000
based on.

00:36:55.000 --> 00:36:55.750
Thank you, Etsuji.

00:36:58.850 --> 00:37:00.980
So we had the idea.

00:37:00.980 --> 00:37:04.640
And the next thing we had
to do was run some tests

00:37:04.640 --> 00:37:07.430
with multiple model
algorithms and find which

00:37:07.430 --> 00:37:10.950
one is the best for our system.

00:37:10.950 --> 00:37:18.790
So the next thing we chose
to do a Google PSO project.

00:37:18.790 --> 00:37:22.260
Netmarble and the
PSO team ran MVM

00:37:22.260 --> 00:37:24.990
about different model
algorithms in parallel,

00:37:24.990 --> 00:37:29.040
such as statistical model,
LSTM deep autoencoder,

00:37:29.040 --> 00:37:33.310
and stacked denoising
autoencoder.

00:37:33.310 --> 00:37:36.130
With the results
from each team, we

00:37:36.130 --> 00:37:39.550
were able to choose the best
model that fits our system.

00:37:43.020 --> 00:37:45.750
There were also many
supports from the Google

00:37:45.750 --> 00:37:50.580
technical account managers and
customer engineers like Jason.

00:37:50.580 --> 00:37:55.140
And also, we had BespinGlobal,
our GCP partners.

00:37:55.140 --> 00:37:59.730
They were there to help us when
there was any problem with GCP.

00:37:59.730 --> 00:38:01.515
And also, they
shared some updates

00:38:01.515 --> 00:38:05.880
so that we can prepare
for the update.

00:38:05.880 --> 00:38:09.000
And also, they
provided many supports

00:38:09.000 --> 00:38:13.350
so we could have a smooth
collaboration with Google.

00:38:16.090 --> 00:38:18.760
As a result, we
finished our product

00:38:18.760 --> 00:38:21.860
successfully and efficiently.

00:38:21.860 --> 00:38:23.920
What I mean by
efficiently is that we

00:38:23.920 --> 00:38:29.670
only used few human
resources and also few time.

00:38:29.670 --> 00:38:35.070
Let's check the model results
as a developer's perspective

00:38:35.070 --> 00:38:37.072
and also user's perspective.

00:38:39.730 --> 00:38:44.080
This is a graph showing
number of users daily.

00:38:44.080 --> 00:38:45.610
As you can see on
the graph, there's

00:38:45.610 --> 00:38:48.190
a light blue graph,
which is showing

00:38:48.190 --> 00:38:52.820
the number of suspicious
users detected by the model.

00:38:52.820 --> 00:38:55.060
The dark blue ones
are the ones who

00:38:55.060 --> 00:39:00.130
are blocked by the
operational team.

00:39:00.130 --> 00:39:04.360
So as you can see, most
of the users detected

00:39:04.360 --> 00:39:08.350
were investigated by
the operational team,

00:39:08.350 --> 00:39:12.910
and they were turned out to
be abusers using hack tools.

00:39:12.910 --> 00:39:15.810
So they were blocked from
playing the game again.

00:39:20.030 --> 00:39:23.570
This is a graph showing the
number of abusers reporting

00:39:23.570 --> 00:39:27.540
posts on the public web page.

00:39:27.540 --> 00:39:31.850
So if normal users see abusers
when they are playing game,

00:39:31.850 --> 00:39:36.650
they put some post to
complain about the abusers

00:39:36.650 --> 00:39:40.390
or to request us to block them.

00:39:40.390 --> 00:39:46.490
They will get angry if they see
the abusers winning so easily.

00:39:46.490 --> 00:39:51.170
So the number of abuser
reports used to be that high.

00:39:51.170 --> 00:39:55.250
But once the anomaly
detection was released,

00:39:55.250 --> 00:39:59.570
the number on the graph
dropped down steeply.

00:39:59.570 --> 00:40:03.200
This happened because the
coverage of the ML model

00:40:03.200 --> 00:40:06.560
was much larger than the
original rule based model

00:40:06.560 --> 00:40:10.400
that Jason has explained.

00:40:10.400 --> 00:40:13.580
So that saved for the
challenges we had.

00:40:13.580 --> 00:40:15.840
And let's move on
to the next part.

00:40:18.990 --> 00:40:22.950
So from now on, let's
find out about things we

00:40:22.950 --> 00:40:24.200
are going to doing the future.

00:40:27.400 --> 00:40:31.630
Until now in Netmarble, we
were running many AI projects

00:40:31.630 --> 00:40:34.690
besides the anomaly detection.

00:40:34.690 --> 00:40:36.280
There's ad fraud
detection, which

00:40:36.280 --> 00:40:38.920
is detecting some
fraud information sent

00:40:38.920 --> 00:40:41.160
by the ad company.

00:40:41.160 --> 00:40:44.980
There's ROAS prediction,
which is predicting

00:40:44.980 --> 00:40:47.050
a return on ad spent.

00:40:47.050 --> 00:40:49.150
There's gold farming
detection, which

00:40:49.150 --> 00:40:52.450
is detecting users who
run thousands of posts

00:40:52.450 --> 00:40:55.210
to collect real game resources.

00:40:55.210 --> 00:40:59.200
There's test automation,
which is using AI model

00:40:59.200 --> 00:41:02.830
to run a game function test.

00:41:02.830 --> 00:41:05.110
There's auto balance
evaluation, which

00:41:05.110 --> 00:41:10.960
is also using AI model to test
the game values automatically.

00:41:10.960 --> 00:41:13.690
And also, last but not
least, the voice command,

00:41:13.690 --> 00:41:17.050
which is playing game
automatically using the user's

00:41:17.050 --> 00:41:19.560
voice.

00:41:19.560 --> 00:41:24.000
Well, in the future, we are
planning to do more and more AI

00:41:24.000 --> 00:41:25.110
projects.

00:41:25.110 --> 00:41:29.890
We are going to extend AI
to all of our businesses.

00:41:29.890 --> 00:41:31.570
And we are going to
enhance partnerships

00:41:31.570 --> 00:41:33.750
with Google, of course.

00:41:33.750 --> 00:41:37.260
And we are going to do more
collaboration and cooperation

00:41:37.260 --> 00:41:42.240
with Industry University and
also Japan and North America AI

00:41:42.240 --> 00:41:44.410
companies.

00:41:44.410 --> 00:41:46.320
So that's it for
the Netmarble side,

00:41:46.320 --> 00:41:51.138
and let's hear more about
the technical side by Jason.

00:41:51.138 --> 00:41:54.470
[APPLAUSE]

00:41:59.420 --> 00:42:03.470
JASON BAEK: Thank you, Jennifer
for the [? best ?] messages

00:42:03.470 --> 00:42:05.990
for us.

00:42:05.990 --> 00:42:11.060
So we are still very closely
working with Netmarble

00:42:11.060 --> 00:42:15.480
in order to enhance their
ML models and ML pipelines.

00:42:15.480 --> 00:42:22.170
So we are trying to do some
technical challenges still.

00:42:22.170 --> 00:42:26.600
So the first thing is that we
are collecting labeled data.

00:42:26.600 --> 00:42:28.640
As Etsuji mentioned,
it's really hard

00:42:28.640 --> 00:42:34.470
to collect labeled data in
reality because no abusers,

00:42:34.470 --> 00:42:37.790
they don't want to get seen.

00:42:37.790 --> 00:42:40.470
Yeah, they just want to hide.

00:42:40.470 --> 00:42:44.840
So in order to leverage
the current models,

00:42:44.840 --> 00:42:49.550
the detecting power is
increased up to 10 plus times.

00:42:49.550 --> 00:42:52.710
So we are collecting
more blocked users.

00:42:52.710 --> 00:42:56.480
And we are just
thinking, what if we

00:42:56.480 --> 00:43:01.110
can leverage those kind of
blocked users as labels?

00:43:01.110 --> 00:43:04.460
So if we can leverage
those kind of blocked

00:43:04.460 --> 00:43:07.220
users and their features,
and user behaviors,

00:43:07.220 --> 00:43:11.760
then we can develop the
supervised learning models.

00:43:11.760 --> 00:43:14.790
So autoencoder is based on
the unsupervised learning

00:43:14.790 --> 00:43:17.580
by clustering some
player behaviors.

00:43:17.580 --> 00:43:21.540
So we're also trying to
leverage the supervised model

00:43:21.540 --> 00:43:25.320
by leveraging blocked
user information.

00:43:25.320 --> 00:43:28.560
And second, the challenge
is that we are still

00:43:28.560 --> 00:43:30.690
revamping ML pipelines.

00:43:30.690 --> 00:43:34.050
So as Jennifer mentioned,
all the ML pipeline

00:43:34.050 --> 00:43:36.670
is based on the many solutions.

00:43:36.670 --> 00:43:41.370
But Netmarble chairman
is very satisfied

00:43:41.370 --> 00:43:43.350
with the performance
of current models,

00:43:43.350 --> 00:43:49.140
so he's encouraging to
deploy their ML model

00:43:49.140 --> 00:43:52.380
to new releasing gaming titles.

00:43:52.380 --> 00:43:55.170
It means that they have
to manage more than 10

00:43:55.170 --> 00:43:58.710
plus ML models in this year.

00:43:58.710 --> 00:44:01.140
In order to support
this, we are trying

00:44:01.140 --> 00:44:05.160
to migrate their ML pipeline
to Kubeflow pipeline

00:44:05.160 --> 00:44:08.790
to accelerate the
model development.

00:44:08.790 --> 00:44:13.230
And the last part is that we are
trying to do ensemble learning.

00:44:13.230 --> 00:44:17.820
So as Etsuji mentioned, we are
not only using single model,

00:44:17.820 --> 00:44:20.430
but also we are leveraging
rule based model.

00:44:20.430 --> 00:44:22.500
And we are trying to use
the supervised learning

00:44:22.500 --> 00:44:27.870
model and the other models
to empower the detection.

00:44:27.870 --> 00:44:31.540
So we are trying to
use ensemble learning.

00:44:31.540 --> 00:44:35.620
So hopefully, next
year, I'm going

00:44:35.620 --> 00:44:40.030
to give you more detail
about our challenges.

00:44:40.030 --> 00:44:41.100
So that's it.

00:44:41.100 --> 00:44:43.180
Thanks for having this session.

00:44:43.180 --> 00:44:47.220
And I want to give you one more
thing is that your feedback is

00:44:47.220 --> 00:44:48.420
highly appreciated.

00:44:48.420 --> 00:44:53.340
So before you leave the stage,
please complete the survey

00:44:53.340 --> 00:44:57.500
on the Cloud Next app.

00:44:57.500 --> 00:44:58.440
Thank you, everyone.

00:44:58.440 --> 00:44:59.810
[KOREAN] Thank you.

00:44:59.810 --> 00:45:01.310
[APPLAUSE]

00:45:01.310 --> 00:45:04.660
[MUSIC PLAYING]

